Z||000000||x cuda man||12-10-1999||11:30 AM||||im new to fieros,i just bought a (86 se) im going to fix up,yesterday i found a sky blue gt in a yard with grass up to the top of the tires,it looked pretty good from the road,can somebody tell me what this car would be worth if fixed up. thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:04 PM||||All depends on whether it was a fastback or a notchback.  If it was the factory original paint, it would be an '87 since that was the only year for the blue.  Can I assume that you meant that it was a fastback since you said it was a GT?  Remember that they made Notchback GTs in '85 and the 1st half of '86.  Anyhow, if it was an '87 GT and you completely restored it (down to the last nut and bolt), you could probably get pretty close to 10 grand out of it if you didn't mind waiting til the right buyer came along.  If all you want to do is make it presentable and drivable, you could easily get $5500 or so out of it.  But remember that it might cost you more than that to fix it up.  It all depends what's wrong with it.  Let us know what it needs and exactly how far you want to go with it and you can get a better answer.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||12-10-1999||02:01 PM||||10 grand for an 87 GT? <BR>Is that in American $? <BR>If it is than I respectfully disagree with 10 grand for anything but a ridiculously low miler. Waiting for someone to put down 10 large for a restored Fiero, in my humble personal opinion, a waste of a good car. You might catch someone who doesn't know better but I have yet to actually hear of even the 200 mile Fiero GT going for $16,000 being sold. The chances of a low miler out in the field are pretty slim. Granted, the blue GT fastback is rare but "collector cars" Fieros are not. The 88 GTs are still the top of the heap, but not by much. MR2 of the same years still get more than we do!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Top to bottom restoration, pristine Fiero could catch $5500 maybe even $6000  but trying to get a profit out of that would be a trick.<P>If you get save that Fiero GT, put it back together for love of the car. If you do it for profit you might be a little disappointed. Check the Kelly Blue book and Car Trader to see what Fieros are going for. And if you want to be depressed than check out the same years for MR2s. I did. (Its not fair!)<P>Please excuse my ranting but I just totally, regretfully have to disagree with anybody who thinks that the Fiero is some sort of "Collector's Car." It just ain't going to happen.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||08:33 PM||||WKDFIERO--I never said anything about the Fiero being a "collector car" (although I'd like to collect as many as possible!).  It sounded like he wanted to fix up the car to sell.  Why else would he ask what it would be worth?  If he correctly restored it, it would be a low mileage car except for the odometer reading.  All original or NOS parts, all hoses, sensors, nuts, bolts, etc new or perfect originals.  I'm sure he could get 10 large out of it.  (And I was talking about US dollars.)  I wasn't sure of his intentions.  That's why I said what I did in my last sentence.  And you're absolutely correct that it would be a waste of a good car to wait for the right buyer, but like I said, I wasn't sure if he was interested in making money by reselling it or if he wanted a good driver for himself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||superXmotorsports||12-10-1999||09:17 PM||||Clean 87 GT's are going for $4500 - $6000 in my part of the world.  I have a 88 GT t-top, 1 owner, 67k miles, that I still haven't sold at $6900!  Ron Smigla on this site found a 88 GT 10k miles for $7000.  So $10000 may be a little inflated for a 87.  I personally think ANY fastback is worth a restoration of somekind.  SXM.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GT||12-10-1999||09:34 PM||||I think any fastback GT fiero that is restored from ground up could get up to 10,000.  Try buying a new car for 10,000.  If you do, you get a piece of junk.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||12-10-1999||09:43 PM||||Superx.... If I would have known about your<BR>t-tops last may, I would have bought it! I see that as a great price. I missed out on 3<BR>seperate t-tops deals last may, because I was not fast enough. Black 88gt t-top 14K miles sold for $12,000.  Red 88gt t-top 49K<BR>sold for $8,000 and a another red 88gt t-top<BR>with about 25k sold big money (i don't recall<BR>how much on this one). I paid $12,900 for my<BR>88gt sunroof car with 485 miles on it, and fully documented. There is big money out there.  I found all of those cars on classifieds2000.com. They will even e-mail you when any new add is posted for a fiero.<BR>Ross<P>BTW x cuda man: I too am a x 68 cuda man, became frustrated and switched to fiero's||||reg||1
Z||000007||superXmotorsports||12-10-1999||11:25 PM||||87GT - I agree that a fully restored Fiero at $10,000 is MUCH better than what 10 grand will buy for a new car these days but it's still a lofty number, esp to a non-Fiero enthusiast.  There was a big discussion about inflated prices on GT's on this forum a couple months ago and they weren't even talking these types of numbers!  I sold the GTR, which had a new 3.2L stroker w/ lots of performance items, new GM super red paint, 16 X 8 triple chrome wheels, poly bushings, ZR tires, only 37k miles on body, etc, etc for $7000 and it took 2 months to sell at that price, orginal asking price was $8500 and I only got 1 call at that number.  SXM.<BR>||||reg||3
